# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
BLOCKS = {
  "base": [
    ("Why 6 inches of base isn't automatic under a driveway near {city}",
     "<p>ICPI's own spec sets a residential driveway base at a minimum of 6 inches of compacted aggregate over well-drained soil, while a patio or walkway only needs about 4 inches. That minimum isn't the end of the math: on wet or poorly drained ground, the same spec calls for 2 to 4 inches more before the base is considered done. Around {city}, which number applies depends on what's actually under the topsoil, not on habit. A crew that quotes 6 inches for every lot skipped the step where someone checks the soil, and that's the step that keeps a driveway from settling at the edges a year later.</p>"),
    ("What a 98 percent Proctor reading actually means for a {city} driveway",
     "<p>ICPI calls for the compacted subgrade and the aggregate base under a driveway to reach at least 98 percent of standard Proctor density, tested against ASTM D698, with vehicular areas held to the stricter modified Proctor instead. That number comes from a plate compactor working the base in lifts of 4 to 6 inches, not one pass over the whole excavation. Skipping a lift, or compacting a lift that's too thick, is how a base looks finished on the surface and still settles under {city} traffic within a season. The spec also sets a surface tolerance of plus or minus three-eighths of an inch over 10 feet, tighter than it sounds once pavers sit on top of it.</p>"),
    ("When the subgrade itself needs help before the base goes down",
     "<p>ICPI's spec admits its own 98 percent compaction standard may not be achievable in extremely saturated or very fine soils, and calls for stabilizing the subgrade instead of forcing the number on ground that won't take it. In practice that means dealing with the soil before a single scoop of aggregate base arrives, whether that's letting it dry out, adding a separation layer, or going with a different base depth altogether. A {city} lot with sand over a denser layer behaves differently than one that's sand straight down, and the subgrade step is where that difference gets handled instead of ignored. Skipping it is a common reason a paver driveway starts to dip within a year or two.</p>"),
    ("Why the base gets graded to shed water, not just compacted",
     "<p>A compacted aggregate base that's flat isn't finished if it holds water instead of moving it along. ICPI's spec allows perforated drainage pipe, typically 4 inches, to be set into a wet subgrade before the base goes down, which matters on the flatwoods soils under a lot of the ground around {city} and the wider region. Grading the base to carry water toward the street or a swale, rather than letting it pond under the pavers, is part of the same step as compaction, not a separate one. A base that's dense but graded wrong still ends up soft in one corner by the following rainy season.</p>"),
    ("Ridge sand versus flatwoods: two different subgrades under {city} driveways",
     "<p>Central Florida and the Suncoast sit on two very different subgrade types. Ridge sands such as Candler and Astatula drain excessively, with a water table that can sit 80 inches or deeper, about as forgiving a subgrade as pavers ever see. Flatwoods soils such as Myakka, Florida's official state soil, or Smyrna and EauGallie hold a water table within 18 inches of the surface for part of most years. A driveway base built the same way on both will perform differently: the sandy ridge lot drains the base almost on its own, while the flatwoods lot needs the extra depth the spec allows for wet ground. Knowing which one sits under a given {city} address changes the base plan before anything gets dug.</p>"),
    ("How the rainy season changes a base crew's schedule",
     "<p>Orlando's airport gauge averages 51.45 inches of rain a year and Sarasota-Bradenton's averages 49.05, with most of it landing in a rainy season that typically runs from late May into mid-October. Compacting an aggregate base on soil that's already saturated doesn't get a crew to 98 percent of Proctor density; it gets a base that looks compacted and isn't. That's why timing matters as much as technique on a {city} job scheduled during the wet months: a day lost waiting for the subgrade to drain is cheaper than a base torn out and redone after the pavers are already down. Dry-season jobs skip that wait entirely.</p>"),
    ("Sidewalk base and driveway base are not the same number",
     "<p>ICPI sets a different minimum base depth for a pedestrian area than for a driveway: about 4 inches of compacted aggregate for a sidewalk or patio over well-drained soil, at least 6 inches for a residential driveway carrying cars, and 8 to 10 inches for a residential street. The difference is the load, not the finish on top, so a {city} driveway built to the walkway number will look identical to a correctly built one until a vehicle sits on it regularly. Mixing up the two specs, or carrying a patio crew's habits onto a driveway, is one of the quieter ways a paver driveway falls short of what it should carry.</p>"),
    ("What the water table under a {city} lot has to do with your base",
     "<p>Flatwoods soils common across Central Florida and the Suncoast, including Myakka, Immokalee, Pomona and Felda, carry a seasonal high water table within 18 inches of the surface for part of most years. ICPI's response to wet ground is straightforward: 2 to 4 inches more compacted base than the standard 6-inch driveway minimum, plus drainage pipe where the subgrade needs it. None of that shows once the pavers are laid, which is exactly the issue: a shallow base on a high-water-table lot near {city} can look right for a season before the first low spot appears. Checking the water table before the base goes in costs far less than fixing a sunken section later.</p>"),
  ],
  "build": [
    ("60 mm or 80 mm: picking paver thickness for a driveway",
     "<p>Pavers come in two standard thicknesses for the jobs a homeowner deals with. A 2⅜-inch (60 mm) unit is the standard spec for pedestrian areas and residential driveways under normal car traffic, while a 3⅛-inch (80 mm) unit is reserved for heavier loads, like streets, industrial pavement, or a driveway that regularly carries something bigger than a sedan. The two aren't interchangeable just because they look similar from the sidewalk: the thicker unit spreads load over a larger area and resists point loading better, which matters every time a tire turns in the same spot. For a typical {city} two-car driveway, 60 mm pavers on a properly compacted base is the standard choice; the 80 mm jump is a conversation for anyone with heavier equipment.</p>"),
    ("Why the bedding sand layer is exactly one inch, not an estimate",
     "<p>Between the compacted aggregate base and the pavers themselves sits a layer of bedding sand, screeded to a nominal 1 inch before it's ever compacted. That number isn't a rough guide: too little and the pavers rock on the base; too much and the layer compacts unevenly once traffic starts rolling over it. The joints between individual units are set at no more than one-eighth of an inch, tight enough that the field locks together once dry joint sand is swept in. On a {city} driveway laid course by course, keeping that bedding layer consistent across the whole field is one of the steps that separates a flat surface from one with visible waves within a year.</p>"),
    ("Edge restraint: the part of a paver driveway nobody sees",
     "<p>Every paver driveway needs edge restraint along its full perimeter and anywhere the paving material changes, because without it the field has nothing stopping it from spreading sideways under traffic. The spec allows plastic or aluminum restraint on a flexible base, with steel reserved for rigid bases, and it's specific about where the anchoring spikes go: into the compacted aggregate base, never into the surrounding soil. It also rules out one shortcut: the metal edging sold for planting beds isn't an acceptable substitute, even though it looks similar on a hardware store shelf. Once the pavers and joint sand are down, the restraint disappears from view on a {city} driveway, which is exactly why it's worth getting right the first time.</p>"),
    ("Concrete, brick, permeable or natural stone: what changes in the build",
     "<p>Market pricing for paver driveways runs roughly $10 to $30 per square foot installed, and the spread exists because the unit itself changes more than the installation method does. Concrete pavers sit toward the lower end of that range, brick a step above, and permeable or natural-stone units higher still, largely on material cost rather than labor. A permeable system also changes the base: instead of a fully compacted aggregate layer meant to shed water, it uses an open-graded stone structure built to let water pass through rather than run off a {city} lot entirely. Choosing the material is really choosing which build the crew follows underneath, not just which color shows up at the surface.</p>"),
    ("Joint sand or polymeric sand: what the spec leaves open",
     "<p>ICPI's own spec treats joint sand plainly: dry sand swept into the eighth-inch joints and compacted in place, with sealers or joint sand stabilizers listed as optional rather than required. Polymeric sand is the common stabilizer choice, meant to bind in the joint and resist the ordinary washout that plain sand can see over time, but the spec doesn't mandate it for a standard residential driveway. That leaves it as a real decision for a {city} homeowner rather than a box every crew checks the same way: plain sand costs less and can be swept back in after a pressure wash, while a stabilized joint holds up longer between resweeps but costs more up front. Either way the joint itself keeps the same eighth-inch tolerance.</p>"),
    ("An apron that meets the right-of-way has its own rules",
     "<p>The strip of driveway between the edge of a property and the street, often called the apron, usually sits inside a right-of-way the city or county controls rather than the homeowner. Florida law keeps a local government from requiring a license just to install a driveway, but that exemption covers the license, not the permit: a local government can still require a permit for work that touches its right-of-way. On a {city} project where the apron crosses into that right-of-way, checking which department reviews that specific strip, separate from the rest of the driveway, is worth doing before the base crew ever shows up.</p>"),
    ("Building a paver driveway over an old concrete slab",
     "<p>An existing concrete driveway can sometimes serve as the structural base for a new paver surface instead of being demolished, but it changes the build in ways worth planning for. The slab has to be sound, without the kind of cracking or settlement that would telegraph through a bedding sand layer, and its drainage has to be checked since a solid slab doesn't absorb water the way a compacted aggregate base does. Adding pavers plus bedding sand on top also raises the finished grade, which matters at a garage threshold or anywhere the driveway meets a {city} sidewalk or the street. Skipping demolition can save a step, but only on a slab that earns the shortcut.</p>"),
    ("A driveway built to carry more than a sedan",
     "<p>A standard residential paver driveway is built around the loads a car puts on it, but a boat trailer, an RV, or a loaded work truck asks more of the same surface. ICPI's spec response is layered: a thicker 80 mm paver instead of the usual 60 mm, a base compacted to the stricter modified Proctor standard used for vehicular areas rather than the pedestrian number, and edge restraint that can actually hold the field together under a turning tire. None of those changes are visible once the job is finished, part of why they're easy to skip on a {city} quote that never asks what the driveway will actually carry. Specifying the load up front is cheaper than rebuilding a surface that wasn't built for it.</p>"),
  ],
  "process": [
    ("The order a paver driveway actually goes in",
     "<p>The sequence doesn't vary much from job to job: excavate to the planned depth, compact the subgrade, place and compact the aggregate base in lifts, screed the bedding sand, lay the pavers course by course, cut units at the borders, install edge restraint, then sweep in and compact the joint sand. Each step depends on the one before it holding up, which is why a {city} crew that rushes the base to reach the visually satisfying part, laying pavers, usually pays for it later in an uneven field. Nothing about the order is optional, even though only the last two steps are visible once the driveway is done and swept clean.</p>"),
    ("Why compaction happens in lifts, not one pass",
     "<p>Aggregate base doesn't get compacted as one thick layer; it goes down and gets compacted in lifts of about 4 to 6 inches at a time, run over with a plate compactor heavy enough to do the job, around 7,000 pounds of compaction force. A 6-inch driveway base is at least one full lift, often closer to two depending on the starting grade, and each one gets its own pass before the next layer goes on top. Pressing the whole depth at once from the surface doesn't reach the bottom of the base the same way, which is how a {city} driveway ends up solid on top and soft two inches down, a flaw that doesn't show until a season of traffic and rain finds it.</p>"),
    ("What a crew checks before a single paver goes down",
     "<p>By the time the first paver is set, the base underneath has already been checked against two separate numbers: compaction density, tested to at least 98 percent of standard Proctor for a driveway, and surface tolerance, held to plus or minus three-eighths of an inch over a 10-foot span. Both checks happen before the bedding sand is even screeded, because fixing a low spot in the base is straightforward and fixing one after the pavers are set is not. On a {city} job, that inspection step is the quiet middle of the process, sandwiched between the excavation everyone notices and the finished surface everyone sees, and it's the part that decides how the driveway looks in two years, not two days.</p>"),
    ("Cutting pavers at the edges takes longer than laying the field",
     "<p>Laying full pavers across the open middle of a driveway moves fast once the bedding sand is screeded flat, course after course set tight against the last one. The borders are a different job: every paver that meets an edge restraint, a garage apron, or an angled property line has to be measured and cut to fit, and a {city} driveway with a curved edge or an angled approach can have more border cuts than field pavers in some sections. That work happens with a wet saw, one piece at a time, which is why a crew's pace visibly slows near the edges of a job even though the material and the base underneath haven't changed at all.</p>"),
    ("Joint sand compaction is the last step, not an afterthought",
     "<p>Once every paver is set and cut to fit, dry joint sand gets swept across the whole field and worked down into the eighth-inch joints, then the entire surface gets a final pass with the plate compactor to seat the sand and lock the units together. Skipping this step, or rushing it, leaves a driveway that looks complete but isn't actually interlocked yet; the pavers can still shift individually under a tire until the joints are filled and compacted. For a {city} crew, that final compaction pass is also the point where a low or high paver still shows up and can be reset before the job is called finished, which is harder to do convincingly later.</p>"),
    ("What rain does to a paver job mid-install",
     "<p>A paver driveway under construction is vulnerable to rain at a few specific points: an open excavation can fill and soften the exposed subgrade, a freshly screeded bedding sand layer can wash or rut before pavers cover it, and loose joint sand swept in but not yet compacted can migrate or wash out in a hard downpour. Orlando and Sarasota-Bradenton both average close to 50 inches of rain a year, much of it landing in a rainy season that runs roughly late May through mid-October, so a {city} schedule built during those months has to plan around afternoon storms rather than hope around them. Covering exposed sand and timing the joint-sand step for a dry stretch isn't optional scheduling, it's part of the process.</p>"),
    ("Inspections on a driveway, if the job needs one",
     "<p>Florida law keeps a local government from requiring a license just to install a driveway, but that doesn't remove the permit itself, and a permit usually comes with an inspection tied to it. Projects with a direct contract price over $2,500 also fall under Florida's lien law, which requires a recorded Notice of Commencement before work starts, separate from any building-permit inspection. Whether a specific {city} driveway needs a permit at all depends on the scope, the jurisdiction, and whether the work touches a right-of-way apron, which is worth asking the local building department directly rather than assuming the answer from a different town's rule.</p>"),
    ("Why an overlay job runs differently than a new build",
     "<p>Installing pavers over sound existing concrete skips the excavation and base-compaction steps that take up most of a new driveway's schedule, but it adds steps a fresh build doesn't need: checking the old slab for cracking or settlement that would transfer through the new surface, confirming the slab actually drains, and planning for the finished grade the added layers create at a garage threshold or a {city} sidewalk tie-in. The bedding sand, paver-laying and joint-sand steps stay identical either way. What changes is the front end of the job, where an overlay trades digging for inspecting, and a bad slab underneath can turn what looked like the faster option into the slower one.</p>"),
  ],
  "risks": [
    ("Efflorescence: the white haze that isn't a defect",
     "<p>A whitish, powdery bloom can appear on new concrete pavers within about 60 days of installation, and ICPI's own spec is direct about what it is: a calcium carbonate deposit that does not affect the structural performance or durability of the pavers. It can look alarming on a dark paver especially, and a driveway installed during a dry stretch near {city} may not show it at all until the first real rain of the wet season brings it out. It usually fades with weather and traffic over time and isn't something a sealer is required to fix. Mistaking it for a flaw in the material, rather than a normal early-life process, is the most common false alarm on a new paver driveway.</p>"),
    ("A sunken paver driveway is almost never a sinkhole",
     "<p>Most dips in a paver driveway trace back to the base underneath: a spot that never reached full compaction, a wet area that settled differently than the rest of the lot, or bedding sand that migrated once water found a path through a joint. True sinkhole activity is a separate geological process, and claims for it concentrate heavily in a handful of counties, Hernando, Pasco and Hillsborough chief among them, with Orange County also appearing on the state's list of counties with the most reported claims. For a driveway in or near {city}, a localized dip after a rainy season is far more likely to be ordinary base settlement than ground collapse, though a sudden, sharply defined depression is worth a second look regardless of which county it's in.</p>"),
    ("What happens when joint sand washes out before it sets",
     "<p>Dry joint sand has to be swept in and compacted to actually lock a paver field together, and until that happens it's just loose material sitting in an eighth-inch gap. A heavy downpour that hits before the compaction pass, a real risk during a wet season that runs roughly late May through mid-October, can wash that sand partway out of a {city} driveway before it ever gets the chance to set. The result isn't just cosmetic: without sand holding the joints, individual pavers can shift and rock under tire loads, which accelerates wear at the exact spots where the sand is thinnest. Re-sanding a washed-out joint is a straightforward fix, but it's one more trip that careful sequencing avoids.</p>"),
    ("Tire-turning wear at the same spot, every day",
     "<p>A driveway sees a narrower range of tire motion than a street does: the same vehicle turning into the same garage bay, day after day, concentrates scrubbing force on a small area instead of spreading it across the whole surface. On a standard 60 mm paver sized for normal car traffic, that repeated turning and braking at one spot, especially near a garage apron, can scuff the surface finish and work joint sand loose faster than it does across the rest of a {city} driveway. It's one reason heavier vehicles, or driveways with a tight turning radius, are candidates for the thicker 80 mm paver and the stricter vehicular compaction standard rather than the standard driveway spec.</p>"),
    ("Oil and fluid stains on an unsealed paver surface",
     "<p>Concrete pavers aren't sealed by default under ICPI's spec, which treats sealing as optional rather than a required step, and an unsealed surface takes on an oil or transmission-fluid stain the way any unsealed masonry does: by absorbing it rather than letting it sit on top. A stain that's blotted quickly, before it has time to soak deeper into the paver and the bedding sand underneath, cleans up far more easily than one left to set for days on a {city} driveway. It's one of the practical reasons some owners choose to seal a new driveway sooner than the spec requires, even though there's no fixed interval written into it, rather than waiting until a stain forces the decision.</p>"),
    ("Skipping edge restraint, or installing it wrong",
     "<p>Edge restraint is required along the full perimeter of a paver driveway, and the spec is specific that the anchoring spikes go into the compacted aggregate base, not the surrounding soil, because soil doesn't hold a spike the way a dense base does. A restraint installed into soft dirt, or skipped at a section that looked solid enough on its own, lets the field spread sideways under traffic over time, which shows up as widening joints and pavers creeping outward near the edge of a {city} driveway. The spec also rules out substituting plastic landscape edging meant for flower beds, since it isn't built to resist the lateral force a driveway puts on it.</p>"),
    ("What an HOA can and can't require before you start",
     "<p>An HOA's authority to approve a driveway's look exists only to the extent its declaration or published guidelines actually say so, and Florida law requires that authority to be used reasonably, with a written denial that cites the specific rule being enforced. As of the 2026 statutes, an HOA also can't require a government-issued building permit as a prerequisite just to review a project, which separates the association's own approval step from the city or county's separate permitting process. A {city} homeowner inside an HOA is dealing with two different approvals that don't have to happen in a fixed order, and confusing the two is a common way a paver driveway project stalls before it starts.</p>"),
    ("Live oak roots under or near a paver driveway",
     "<p>A mature live oak's root system can extend well past its canopy, and roots running under a driveway don't stop growing once pavers sit on top of them; a root that thickens over a few years can lift a section of base and the pavers riding on it, usually along a visible seam rather than evenly. Several Central Florida and Sarasota-area cities protect larger live oaks by ordinance, which can limit root pruning or removal near a {city} driveway even when a root is part of the problem. Routing a driveway's base around a known root zone, or accepting a shallower design near a protected tree, is often the more realistic fix than fighting the tree for the space.</p>"),
  ],
  "care": [
    ("Resweeping joint sand after a pressure wash",
     "<p>A pressure washer aimed straight down into the joints of a paver driveway does what rain does in miniature: it can blast loose joint sand out from between the units, especially where the sand was never stabilized with a sealer or a polymeric additive. ICPI's spec treats that stabilizer as optional, which means plenty of {city} driveways run on plain dry sand that needs resweeping after a thorough cleaning, not just an occasional touch-up. Washing at an angle rather than straight down, and keeping the pressure moderate near the joints specifically, slows how often that resweep is needed, but it doesn't eliminate the job on a surface built with the standard, unstabilized joint.</p>"),
    ("Sealing a paver driveway: no fixed clock, but a real one",
     "<p>ICPI's own spec doesn't give a number of years between sealer applications, noting only that it depends on use, climate and the sealer's own quality, with acrylic sealers typically holding up for what the spec calls a few years before recoating is worth it. Climate does real work in that sentence for a {city} driveway: both service areas sit close to the 50-inch mark for annual rainfall, concentrated heavily in a rainy season, and that much water and sun exposure wears a sealer faster than a drier region would. Watching the surface for fading sheen or water that stops beading is a better guide than a calendar, since the spec itself won't give a firm date.</p>"),
    ("Pulling weeds and moss out of the joints",
     "<p>Joint sand that's thinned out from washing or wear leaves an opening for weed seeds and, in a shaded or damp section of a {city} driveway, for moss or algae to take hold between the pavers. Pulling growth by hand or with a joint-safe tool and resweeping fresh sand into the gap afterward keeps the problem from spreading to neighboring joints, since one open seam tends to collect debris that the next one catches too. A driveway with a stabilized or polymeric joint resists this far better than one with plain dry sand, which is one of the trade-offs worth weighing against the lower upfront cost of the standard spec.</p>"),
    ("Resetting a sunken paver, instead of patching it",
     "<p>Because a paver driveway is built from individual units rather than one poured surface, a localized low spot, from a soft patch in the base or sand that migrated after heavy rain, can usually be fixed by lifting the affected pavers, correcting the bedding sand underneath, and resetting the same units rather than pouring a visible patch the way a concrete repair would need. That's a meaningful difference for a {city} homeowner weighing long-term upkeep: the fix addresses the base problem directly instead of covering it, and the repaired section matches the rest of the driveway once the joint sand is swept back in, rather than standing out as an obviously patched spot.</p>"),
    ("What to do about an oil stain, fast",
     "<p>Because concrete pavers aren't sealed by default, an oil or fluid stain soaks in rather than sitting on the surface, so the fastest response, blotting the spill right away rather than wiping it or letting it sit, matters more on an unsealed {city} driveway than it would on a sealed one. A stain that's already set can sometimes be lifted with a degreaser made for masonry, applied and worked in rather than pressure-washed aggressively, since high pressure aimed straight at the joints risks the same sand loss a routine cleaning causes. Sealing the driveway ahead of time, even without a fixed resealing schedule required, is the more direct way to keep future spills from reaching the paver at all.</p>"),
    ("Keeping sprinklers off the paver surface",
     "<p>A sprinkler head aimed across a driveway instead of at the lawn keeps a strip of pavers damp well after the surrounding ground has dried, exactly the kind of shaded, constantly wet condition that lets moss and algae take hold in the joints. It's worth checking head direction and spray pattern on any {city} property where a zone borders the driveway, particularly since both the Orlando-area and Suncoast water districts restrict watering to specific days and hours, so a misaimed head is often running on a schedule nobody is watching closely. Redirecting or swapping the head is a small fix compared with the joint maintenance a chronically wet strip ends up needing.</p>"),
    ("Checking edge restraint once a year",
     "<p>Edge restraint sits below grade and out of sight once a driveway is finished, which makes it easy to forget until the joints along the edge start widening or pavers near the perimeter begin creeping outward under traffic. A quick yearly check, pressing along the border by hand or foot near {city} to feel for movement, catches a loosening spike or a section where landscaping work accidentally disturbed the restraint long before the problem reaches the field of pavers. Since the spikes anchor into the compacted base rather than the soil, restraint that's failing usually means something shifted at that connection, not that the whole system needs replacing, which keeps the fix smaller if it's caught early.</p>"),
    ("What efflorescence needs from you: nothing",
     "<p>The whitish bloom that can show up on new concrete pavers within the first couple of months doesn't call for a cleaner, a sealer, or a change in routine, because ICPI's spec is clear that it doesn't affect the structural performance of the pavers at all. It tends to fade on its own with normal weather and traffic, and scrubbing at it or sealing over it early can trap it rather than help it clear, so the honest advice for a {city} driveway showing this in its first season is to keep up the normal sweeping and let it wear off. Treating it as a maintenance problem instead of a normal early-life process is the only real mistake available here.</p>"),
  ],
}
